're gonna go in that meeting  and not only are you gonna get fired but  your boss is gonna tell you to your face  you're worthless you're a loser you will  never work again and then you start  thinking about your family and your  family's got starved and probably your  younger  they're gonna die too because you will  never get another job  that's anxiety that's when your  automatic thoughts of I should have been  on time and I'm have to pay the piper on  this one boom goes off to the races so I  want you to write down your thoughts and  do a quick inventory is it true or my  making it up is it real or not real and  do a quick inventory of that list okay  number two make a list of what you can  control and what you cannot control and  let the things you can't control  go can you control showing up on time  yep  can you control going to your boss and  saying I'm sorry here are the three  things I've put in place that I will  never be late again  can you go home to your wife or to your  husband or to your kids and say dad's  got a problem being late and I'm sorry  and here's the things I'm gonna do to  never be late again  and I'm gonna need y'all's help in that  as I transition in my life yeah you can  control those things can you control if  your boss just decides enough is enough  you're fired  no can you control if you're gonna get a  job tomorrow or the next day or the next  day no and so worrying about those  things right now  not helpful worry about the things you  can control and let the other things and  go number three take control of your  body  this may sound cheesy but research  suggests my life supports this idea of  taking deep breaths and controlling our  breathing stopping personally I follow  box breathing inhale for four seconds  hold for four seconds let it out for  four seconds just chill for four seconds  and then you breathe it in again  some folks say do that for two minutes  three minutes five minutes but  controlling your parasympathetic nervous  system slow it down  okay also before you ever get to this  anxious moment taking care of your body  like exercising eating right getting a  full night of sleep those are things you  do to keep your kitchen from ever  catching fire in the first place so that  when you it's like building another  outlet so you don't to load everything  up right take care of your body  number four connect with a friend or  group now when your alarm starts  spinning don't text don't email call go  see somebody face to face our bodies are  designed to Co regulate themselves with  other people I wish it wasn't the case I  wish I could be the Lone Ranger  maybe I'm from Texas dude I wish we  could all just pull ourselves up by our  bootstraps and figure everything out on  our own we can't it's a lie you've got  to have other people so when you know  the anxiety alarms are starting to spin  up you can hear them getting louder and  louder you may not always be able to see  the smoke in your kitchen you may not be  able to see which rooms are on fire call  somebody talk to somebody bring somebody  into your home and say I need some  support I need some help my alarm is  about to go off again  they're starting to sound you gotta have  other people and number five is  gratitude  every day but especially when your  alarms are starting to kick off take  that same piece of paper that you wrote  your thoughts and feelings down on take  that same piece of paper that you wrote  what you can control what you can't  control flip it over and make a  gratitude journal quickly write down  five sentences that begin with the words  I am grateful for I am grateful for my  family I am grateful for my wife I'm  grateful for my job I'm grateful for my  health whatever it may be I keep my  gratitude journal with me all the time  every day and I know that sounds crazy  but the research shows people with  gratitude people who daily express  gratitude our healthier have better  relationships they sleep better 